{"data":{"attachmentkeynames":["rs"],"desc":"\"Master moved - bring my branch up to date.\" fetch --prune -> merge origin/<default> (master, else main; the local default when there is no origin) into the current branch -> push when the branch tracks a remote. The verb pull cannot do this: it targets the branch's own upstream, which for a solo developer never moves. Requires that the repo is ON `branch`, no conflicts, no merge in flight; a fetch failure merges nothing. A dirty tree is not a blocker: git refuses a merge that would overwrite an edit (reported as refused, nothing merged) and untouched edits ride along, reported as `carried`. Already-current is an ok no-op (merged 0). A real conflict is reported with the tree left mid-merge on the branch - resolve then commit, or merge --abort - never half-merged. Returns {status, msg, branch, base, merged (commit count), pushed, push_err, carried, steps}.","import":"use ndata::dataarray::DataArray;","params":[{"name":"repo","type":"String"},{"name":"branch","type":"String"}],"returntype":"FLAT","type":"rust"},"id":"wrwzmq1a0733d8548hb","readers":[],"time":1788647672846,"username":"system","writers":[]}